        difficulty: { level: 'Very high', text: "Port land is scarce and allotted by port authorities, hazardous-cargo terminals need long safety and environmental approvals, and cryogenic LPG and ammonia tanks are specialised builds. Few rivals can add capacity quickly." },
        moat: "Scarce port land, modern LPG terminals that turn cargo 70–100 times a year against 26–30 at older sites (per management), and pipeline and rail links make it hard to bypass. The weakness is pricing: LPG fees are fixed per tonne and did not rise even in a shortage, so growth depends on adding volume and capacity.",
        salesDrivers: ["**India's LPG imports**: it aims to grow LPG volume at least 25% a year from about 10% of national demand, taking share from older, slower terminals; volumes were back to 80–85% of normal by July.", "**New capacity**: the ₹1,675cr JNPA expansion (318,100 cbm of liquids, 77,236 tonnes of LPG and a bottling plant), a new 52,000-tonne LPG tank at JNPA, 94,148 cbm at Kandla, 49,577 cbm at Kochi by early FY28 and possibly 60,000 cbm at Mangalore.", "**Pipeline and rail links**: four cross-country LPG pipeline connections this year raise throughput per tank.", "**New markets**: ammonia (an MoU with L&T at Kandla), a possible role worth about ₹20,000cr at the planned Vadhvan port, inland depots, national strategic storage and industrial terminals, within a $5bn capex goal by 2030–31."],
        inputs: "**Mostly insulated from materials.** It never owns the cargo. Operating costs are power and terminal running costs (11% of sales), staff (6%) and other costs (9%); the big charges sit below operating profit, in depreciation (₹208cr in FY26) and interest (₹110cr) on a fast-growing asset base.",
        costEffect: "**Mostly insulated.** Fees are per tonne or per cubic metre and unrelated to product prices, so the margin has stayed in the 70s. **What happened:** the operating margin rose from 65% in FY23 to 74% in FY26 (Screener) and 76.7% in Q1 FY27 as new tanks filled and fixed costs were spread. Net profit is much thinner: ₹342cr on ₹923cr of revenue in FY26 after depreciation, interest and tax, and the building programme keeps both depreciation and interest rising.",
        competitors: [{ name: "State oil companies' own LPG import terminals", note: 'Older terminals that turn cargo only 26–30 times a year, per management.' }, { name: 'Other liquid tank-farm operators', note: 'Such as Ganesh Benzoplast at JNPT, competing for chemical storage at the same ports.' }, { name: 'New port developers', note: 'Compete for land and projects at new ports such as Vadhvan.' }],
        position: "**A leading LPG and liquid terminal operator in India**, handling roughly 10% of national LPG demand by management's estimate, at 7 of India's more than 200 ports.",
        money: "Screener shows **₹3,424cr of net debt**, about five times a year's operating profit. The ₹10,000cr first phase should be finished by March 2027 (June 2027 at worst), and the $5bn plan is to be funded by debt (management caps gearing at 0.6 and debt at 3.5 times EBITDA, though Screener's figures put it above that today), internal cash and new equity. In FY26 it bought about ₹1,700cr of terminals from its own promoter group (Mangalore for ₹1,040cr and Pipavav for ₹658cr) and revalued plant up by ₹405cr; the auditors made this a key audit matter. On its own measure net debt was 2.64 times operating profit at March. Promoters hold **87%** and must bring this down to 75% by around June 2028 (the date is unclear in the transcript), which means a large share sale. The dividend is tiny.",
        watch: ["LPG volumes returning to at least 25% growth.", "The first 100,000 cbm at JNPA in Q3 FY27 and ₹10,000cr of assets by March 2027.", "The size and price of the promoter share sale due by 2028."],
