        moat: "Scale, cheap group feedstock, biomass-fired boilers and dual feed make it a low-cost producer. But the government sets the price and the oil companies set the volume, so low cost helps it survive a glut rather than earn more.",
        salesDrivers: ["**The annual tender**: it got 24.5 crore litres for 2025-26, lifted to about 40 crore with 10 crore litres after the Supreme Court judgment in August and 5 crore more from the oil companies (Q2 call); 8.5 crore litres were still to supply in October–November. For 2026-27 (November to October) it hopes for 40–42 crore litres against capacity of about 55 crore.", "**What the new tender allows**: the MD said in the Q2 call that it bars cane juice and B-heavy molasses, so sugar-only distilleries drop out; press reports put the maize price at ₹68.86 a litre, ₹3 less than last year, but the tender document has not been seen.", "**Higher blending**: India reached 20% ethanol in petrol in 2025-26; fuel standards for E22–E30 exist, but a roadmap beyond 20% is still awaited.", "**Biogas plants**: ten new plants with Sumitomo and GAIL should add revenue from Q4 FY27, ramping to about 85% use in FY28.", "**Jet fuel and exports**: the Andhra Pradesh plant could use 20 crore litres a year of its own ethanol from FY29; ethanol exports are being discussed but are not yet allowed."],
        inputs: "**Maize, broken rice and cane molasses and syrup**, much of the molasses from its promoters' Nirani Sugars (₹879cr of purchases in FY26). Maize bought at ₹17–22 a kg gave about ₹15–16 a litre of contribution; at ₹25.5 a kg it is ₹6–7. Materials were 65% of sales in FY26.",
